# Scope Intelligence — rules for Claude Code

This repo has a pre-built scope index at `.scope-intelligence/`.
ALWAYS query it before reading source files, scanning directories, or making
any edit. Every coding task must follow the phases below.

---

## Phase 1 — Orient (run before touching anything)

```
scope summary                        # repo size, languages, top features
scope inventory --repo .             # file/class/symbol roster without reading source
scope mem fetch --feature <name>     # past decisions, bugs, patterns for this feature
scope doc fetch-for <name>           # design-doc context for this feature
```

Use `inventory` as your first file-map. It shows every class and symbol in the
repo without spending tokens on source bodies. Only fetch source after inventory
tells you which files matter.

If `.ai-context/compact/` exists, read compact sidecars first:
  - Sidecars are at `.ai-context/compact/generated/*.scope`
  - Read the DSL block; fetch the original only when full detail is needed.

---

## Phase 2 — Scope the task (pick the right query for the task type)

**Bug fix**
```
scope feature <feature>              # identify the relevant feature
scope symbol <failing-symbol>        # callers + callees of the broken symbol
scope impacted --symbol <name>       # blast radius before touching it
scope tests --feature <feature>      # exact test set to run
```

**New feature / adding code**
```
scope features                       # check if a related feature already exists
scope touchpoints                    # routes, config keys, DB models, events to be aware of
scope inventory --feature <nearest>  # nearest existing feature's file/symbol roster
scope graph <feature> --kind deps    # dependency graph — see what you'd plug into
scope mem fetch --feature <name>     # prior decisions and gotchas
```

**Refactor / rename / move**
```
scope impacted --symbol <name>       # full transitive blast radius BEFORE touching
scope callers <name>                 # every call site
scope callees <name>                 # every dependency of this symbol
scope graph <name> --kind calls      # visual call graph
scope tests --feature <feature>      # tests to keep green
```

**Dependency / import change**
```
scope impacted --file <path>         # who imports this file transitively
scope touchpoints                    # check if file is a route, config, or schema
scope diff HEAD~1                    # what else changed recently in this area
```

**Test writing**
```
scope tests --feature <feature>      # existing test coverage — avoid duplication
scope symbol <name>                  # understand the symbol's contract before mocking
scope inventory --feature <feature> --no-symbols   # file roster only, fast
```

**Architecture / design work**
```
scope graph <feature> --kind deps    # Mermaid dependency graph
scope graph <symbol> --kind calls    # call graph for a key entry point
scope doc search "<topic>"           # search design docs for prior decisions
scope doc fetch-for <feature>        # full design-doc context for this area
```

---

## Phase 3 — Code

- Read ONLY files returned by Phase 2 queries.
- Do NOT glob or `ls` the repo. Do NOT open files not in scope query results.
- If a file seems relevant but wasn't returned, run `scope impacted` or
  `scope symbol` on it before opening it — the index may already explain why.
- Make the smallest change that satisfies the task. One feature, one concern.

---

## Phase 4 — Verify

```
scope tests --feature <feature>       # get the exact test command to run
scope impacted --file <changed-file>  # confirm no unexpected blast radius
```

Run only the tests returned by `scope tests`. Do not run the full suite unless
`scope impacted` shows cross-feature impact.

---

## Phase 5 — Update index and capture memory

```
scope update --repo . --files <every file you changed or created>
```

If you discovered something stable and reusable — a bug pattern, an architectural
rule, a non-obvious constraint — capture it:

```
scope mem capture --signal validated-claim --evidence "<what you found>" --feature <name>
```

Or for a richer entry:
```
scope mem add --type semantic --note "<fact>" --feature <name> --confidence 0.9
scope mem add --type bug      --file <path>  --note "<what broke and why>"
scope mem add --type decision --note "<why this approach was chosen>"
```

---

## Hard rules

- NEVER scan the whole repo with find/glob/ls before querying scope.
- NEVER open a file just to understand what it does — use `scope symbol` or
  `scope inventory` first.
- NEVER skip `scope impacted` before editing a shared utility, base class,
  or widely-imported module.
- If scope returns no results for a query, THEN fall back to source reads —
  and immediately run `scope update` afterwards so the index learns the new file.
- After any bulk edit (rename, move, delete), always run `scope index .` to
  rebuild, not `scope update` — update is for single-file incremental changes.

---

## When scope cannot answer

Escalation ladder — stop at the first level that answers:
1. `scope inventory` — shape of repo, file/class/symbol list
2. `scope feature` / `scope symbol` — focused source context
3. `scope doc fetch-for` / `scope doc search` — design intent
4. `scope mem fetch` / `scope mem search` — past lessons
5. Read source directly — only after all above are exhausted

---

Stored data is JSON/JSONL only — no source code bodies. Safe to commit.
